From: Kernel Investigation <kinv@fb.com>
Date: Tue, 30 Sep 2026 00:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] btrfs: bound root->inodes lock hold in the extent map shrinker

The extent map shrinker's find_first_inode_to_shrink() takes the per-root
root->inodes XArray lock once and then walks the inode xarray looking for an
inode that has loaded extent maps, skipping inodes with no extent maps
(RB_EMPTY_ROOT) or that fail write_trylock()/igrab(). The only release point
inside the loop is cond_resched_lock(&root->inodes.xa_lock), which drops the
lock ONLY when a reschedule is already pending.

root->inodes.xa_lock is extremely hot: every inode creation
(xa_reserve()/xa_store() from btrfs_create_new_inode()) and every inode
eviction (btrfs_del_inode_from_root(), driven heavily by memcg direct
reclaim via prune_icache_sb()) for the subvolume serializes on it. On a busy
CPU where need_resched() stays clear, cond_resched_lock() never drops the
lock, so the shrinker can hold it while scanning a very large number of
cached inodes on an active subvolume.

We observed this as a soft lockup / panic in production: a 192-vCPU KVM
guest (which falls back to the unfair virt_spin_lock() test-and-set path
because PV spinlocks are not active) had ~20 vCPUs spinning on one
root->inodes.xa_lock while the extent-map shrinker kworker held it
mid-scan. drgn showed the holding scan had advanced ~772M in ino-space
within a single uninterrupted lock hold. A file-creating task
(btrfs_create_new_inode -> xa_store) starved for >22s and softlockup_panic
converted it to a panic.

The sibling loop btrfs_scan_inode() already bails on contention
(rwlock_needbreak(&tree->lock)); find_first_inode_to_shrink() has no
equivalent bound on the far hotter xa_lock. Force a drop of the lock every
BTRFS_EM_SHRINK_INODES scanned inodes so other users always make progress,
independent of the preemption model or spinlock fairness. The scan resumes
from 'from', so no inodes are skipped or revisited incorrectly.

Fixes: 102044384056 ("btrfs: make the extent map shrinker run asynchronously as a work queue job")
Signed-off-by: Kernel Investigation <kinv@fb.com>
---
 fs/btrfs/extent_map.c | 28 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/fs/btrfs/extent_map.c b/fs/btrfs/extent_map.c
index 02bfdb976e40..ee0fe4e1f65f 100644
--- a/fs/btrfs/extent_map.c
+++ b/fs/btrfs/extent_map.c
@@ -1191,11 +1191,18 @@ static long btrfs_scan_inode(struct btrfs_inode *inode, struct btrfs_em_shrink_c
 	return nr_dropped;
 }

+/*
+ * Maximum number of inodes find_first_inode_to_shrink() scans while holding the
+ * root->inodes lock before dropping it to let other users make progress.
+ */
+#define BTRFS_EM_SHRINK_INODES 100
+
 static struct btrfs_inode *find_first_inode_to_shrink(struct btrfs_root *root,
 						      u64 min_ino)
 {
 	struct btrfs_inode *inode;
 	unsigned long from = min_ino;
+	unsigned int scanned = 0;

 	xa_lock(&root->inodes);
 	while (true) {
@@ -1234,7 +1241,26 @@ static struct btrfs_inode *find_first_inode_to_shrink(struct btrfs_root *root,
 		write_unlock(&tree->lock);
 next:
 		from = btrfs_ino(inode) + 1;
-		cond_resched_lock(&root->inodes.xa_lock);
+		/*
+		 * The root->inodes lock is heavily contended: every inode
+		 * creation (xa_reserve()/xa_store() from btrfs_create_new_inode())
+		 * and every inode eviction (btrfs_del_inode_from_root()) for the
+		 * subvolume takes it. Holding it while scanning a large number of
+		 * inodes here can stall those tasks for a long time; on
+		 * virtualized hosts that fall back to unfair spinlocks this has
+		 * been observed to trigger multi-second soft lockups. cond_resched_lock()
+		 * only drops the lock when a reschedule is pending, which may never
+		 * happen on a busy CPU, so also force a drop every
+		 * BTRFS_EM_SHRINK_INODES inodes. The scan resumes from 'from'.
+		 */
+		if (++scanned == BTRFS_EM_SHRINK_INODES) {
+			scanned = 0;
+			xa_unlock(&root->inodes);
+			cond_resched();
+			xa_lock(&root->inodes);
+		} else {
+			cond_resched_lock(&root->inodes.xa_lock);
+		}
 	}
 	xa_unlock(&root->inodes);

--
2.43.0
